---
.title = "No LLMs",
.date = @date("2026-06-16T00:00:00"),
.author = "Aven",
.layout = "post.shtml",
.draft = true,
.custom = {},
---

I don't use LLMs to code, and I find it somewhat bizarre
that everyone else does. The reason is simple: I want to have
control over my tools and the projects I make. I want to
be able to work even if I'm using a [$109 Chromebook][5]
flashed with [Mr Chromebox UEFI firmware][4] that
runs [Void Linux][6] while living off-grid in a tent. (Hypothetically...)
It's the same reason I don't use proprietary game engines or IDEs,
but with costs raised exponentially; I'm not sure if there
is a reward great enough for me to pay $200 a month and require an
internet connection and/or
a beefy compute machine just to type some text.

I find it bizarre that "AI" is reviled in every other creative field,
but almost ubiquitously accepted in coding. There are a few projects
that are explicitly against LLM
use, e.g. [Zig][3], but they are extreme outliers.
Coding is an
art with personal expression like any other, I don't see the
difference between slop drawing, slop prose, and slop code. I'm not
even on board with most moral arguments against AI, I just
see that it is ruining everything I like about the world.

